async (el) => {
  const html = document.documentElement;
  const prev = html.style.scrollBehavior;
  // Jump instantly so the measurement is deterministic; we are checking where the
  // click lands and which link lights up, not the animation itself.
  html.style.scrollBehavior = 'auto';

  const bar = document.querySelector('.topbar');
  const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.topbar nav a[href^="#"]')];
  const frame = () => new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(() => requestAnimationFrame(r)));

  const rows = [];
  for (const link of links) {
    const id = link.getAttribute('href').slice(1);
    scrollTo(0, 0);
    await frame();
    link.click();
    await frame();
    await frame();

    const sec = document.getElementById(id);
    const barBottom = bar.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;
    const secTop = sec.getBoundingClientRect().top;
    const activeLink = links.find((a) => a.getAttribute('aria-current') === 'true');
    const activeId = activeLink ? activeLink.getAttribute('href').slice(1) : null;

    // Nothing before the clicked section may still be showing.
    const idx = links.indexOf(link);
    let leftover = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < idx; j++) {
      const r = document.getElementById(links[j].getAttribute('href').slice(1)).getBoundingClientRect();
      leftover = Math.max(leftover, Math.round(r.bottom - barBottom));
    }

    rows.push({
      clicked: id,
      active: activeId,
      ok: activeId === id,
      topRelativeToBar: Math.round(secTop - barBottom),
      headingBelowBar: Math.round(sec.querySelector('h2').getBoundingClientRect().top - barBottom),
      priorLeftoverPx: leftover,
    });
  }

  scrollTo(0, 0);
  html.style.scrollBehavior = prev;
  return JSON.stringify({ rows, failures: rows.filter((r) => !r.ok).length }, null, 1);
}

content.text:
Fixed and committed as `4f8a510`.

## What was wrong

The scrollspy watched a band running from 20% to 45% down the viewport and highlighted whichever section covered the most of it. A section wins that band as soon as its heading clears the upper edge, which is why the nav moved on so early. At one measured point, **226px of Coverage was still on screen** after the bar had already switched to Posture.

## What it does now

It tracks the earliest section still showing below the bar. While any part of a section remains visible, it keeps the highlight; the nav only advances once that section has scrolled off entirely.

That rule also happens to describe where a click lands. Clicking tucks the target's top edge 61px behind the bar, which puts the previous section's bottom edge exactly out of range — so scrolling and clicking now resolve to the same boundary instead of disagreeing.

```2003:2023:docs/liatrio-design-system-showcase.src.html
  /* The current section is the earliest one still showing below the bar, so the
     highlight only advances once the previous section has scrolled away entirely.
     Clicking a link lands the next section's top edge under the bar, which puts
     the previous one exactly out of range, so both routes agree. */
  const pick = () => {
    const barBottom = bar ? bar.getBoundingClientRect().bottom : 0;
    const fold = window.innerHeight;
    const doc = document.documentElement;

    /* The last section plus the footer may be too short to push its predecessor
       off the top, so the end of the page falls back to the deepest one in view. */
    if (window.scrollY + fold >= doc.scrollHeight - 2) {
      const inView = sections.filter((s) => s.getBoundingClientRect().top < fold);
      return inView[inView.length - 1];
    }

    return sections.find((s) => {
      const r = s.getBoundingClientRect();
      return r.bottom > barBottom + 1 && r.top < fold;
    });
  };
```

## Verification

I wrote a test that asserts your rule directly rather than checking my own logic back at itself: at every sampled scroll position, no section *before* the highlighted one may still be on screen. Sampled 121 positions at four viewports (390×740, 1280×520, 1280×900, 1440×1080).

The old build failed at 12 positions. The new build has **zero violations at all four**, every one of the 15 sections gets a contiguous turn, and the last section holds the highlight at the page bottom. A separate test clicked all 15 links and confirmed each still lands with its heading 61px below the bar and the right link lit — the behavior you said was already perfect.

Here's the new build at scroll position 2521, the exact point where the old one had already flipped to Posture:

![nav holding Coverage while its tail is still visible](/tmp/nav-proof.png)

One thing I deliberately left alone: there's no highlight at all while you're in the masthead above the first section, since nothing in the nav corresponds to it. Say the word if you'd rather Coverage light up from the top.

The bottom-of-page fallback is worth knowing about — if the last section plus footer are ever too short to push their predecessor off the top, the strict rule could never reach the final section, so the end of the page falls back to whatever is deepest in view. It isn't triggered at any viewport I tested, but a short landscape phone could hit it.

